## ABSTRACT
Developing advanced porous materials possessing both a high storage capacity and selectivity for acetylene \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\right)\) remains challenging but a sought-after endeavor. Herein we show a strategy involving synergic combination of spatial confinement and commensurate stacking for enhanced \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) storage and capture via maximizing the host–guest and guest–guest interactions. Two ultramicroporous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), MIL-160 and MOF-303 are elaborately constructed to exhibit ultrahigh \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) uptakes of 235 and \(195~\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), respectively, due to the confinement effect of the suitable pore sizes and periodically dispersed molecular recognition sites. Specially, \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) capacity of MIL-160 sets a new benchmark for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) storage. The exceptional separation performances of two materials for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) over both \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and ethylene \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}\right)\), which is rarely observed, outperform most of the benchmark materials for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) capture. We scrutinized the origins of ultrahigh \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) loading in the confined channels via theoretical investigations. The superior separation efficiency for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}\) mixtures with unprecedented \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) trapping capacity \((>200\ L{\cdot}\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\) was further demonstrated by dynamic breakthrough experiments.
## KEYWORDS
adsorption and separation, acetylene storage, commensurate stacking, gas cluster, metal-organic frameworks
## 1 Introduction
The emergence of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers, self-assembled through the coordination of metal nodes to organic linkers, has triggered
Ultramicroporous MOFs (pore size \(<\ 0.7\ \mathrm{\nm}\) ) have been envisaged as a suitable platform to pursue promising materials for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capture [30]. The suitable ultramicropore is supposed to provide a well confined environment for accommodation of multiple \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules and occurrence of the maximization of host–guest and guest–guest interactions, thus enhancing the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake. Great endeavors have been made to develop tailor-made
MOFs for \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capture [3, 31–39], and a high degree of controlling the oriented arrangement regarding the recognition sites and the spatial confinement behavior were proved to be beneficial for the formation of \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) clusters, then leading to high \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capacity. A prominent example is SIFSIX-1-Cu, which possesses high \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capacity \( (190.4\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}) \) and good \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}} \) (50/50) separation selectivity due to the fact that \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) molecules formed "gas clusters" through intermolecular guest–guest interactions within the confined pores (Scheme 1) [22]. However, the potential use of SIFSIX-1-Cu for industrial \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}} \) separation was hampered by its intrinsic features of high cost and low operational stability. Nevertheless, the principle mentioned above opened a powerful approach for solving the trade-off problem. Bearing this in mind, CAU-10-H and CAU-10- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) were reported with an appropriate functionalized pore environment to enable efficient \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) packing, however, the \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) uptakes (89.8 and \( 105.3\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1} \), respectively) are still moderate [40, 41]. Furthermore, to our knowledge, the development of one material that can simultaneously separate \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}} \) and \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}} \) with high efficiency, is rarely reported and still remains a challenge as the above cases merely focused on the separation of either \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}} \) or \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}} \) mixture. To break the current bottleneck for boosting \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) adsorption capacity and selectivity within the confined ultramicropores, exploration of novel adsorption mechanism regarding such as expansion of \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) cluster by reinforcing the multiple host–guest and guest–guest interactions is highly desirable.
Herein, we exquisitely integrate the pore confinement and commensurate stacking mechanism into the tailor-made MOFs for \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capture with ultrahigh capacity (Scheme 1). To exemplify this strategy, two aluminum-based MOFs, MIL-160, and MOF303 with one-dimensional (1D) ultramicroporous channels were constructed with periodically dispersed electronegative binding sites on the pore surfaces. Especially, the commensurate stacking of \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) along the channel axis in MIL-160 was observed to afford a record-high \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) uptake of \( 235\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1} \) at \( 298\;\mathrm{K} \) and 1 bar, equal to 9 molecules of \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}} \) per cell, enabling MIL-160 to outperform the benchmark materials. The specific commensurate stacking and guest–guest interactions between \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) were verified via molecular simulations. Encouragingly, these two MOFs exhibited simultaneously for both equimolar \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}} \) and \( \mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}} \) mixtures. Additionally, MIL-160 and MOF-303 showed excellent stability and can be rapidly obtained via microwave synthesis using cheap materials. In this contribution, not only the perfect
match of multiple binding sites for \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) and pore confinement were realized within the model MOFs, but also the separation potential for industrial applications was demonstrated via dynamic separation process.
## 2 Results and discussion
MIL-160 and MOF-303 are constructed using \( \mathrm{AlCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) and two bent dicarboxylic linkers, 2,5-furandicarboxylic acid (FDC) and 3,5-pyrazoledicarboxylic acid (PZDC), respectively (Fig. 1(a)), via the microwave synthesis method. In order to better activate the pore channel, the two MOFs were treated by methanol exchange for three times. MIL-160 exhibits yfm topology and is constructed by fourfold helical rod-like secondary building units (SBUs) of ciscorner-sharing \( \mathrm{AlO}_{6} \) polyhedra bridged by FDC linkers (Fig. 1(b)). Besides, MOF-303 with xhh topology is built from infinite \( \mathrm{Al(OH)(-COO)_{2}} \) SBUs consisting of alternating cis-trans cornershared \( \mathrm{AlO}_{6} \) octahedra that are connected by PZDC linkers (Fig. 1(c)). The overall three-dimensional (3D) structures of two materials are found to be ultramicroporous in nature having a square-shaped channel with dimension \( 5.0\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times5.0\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) for MIL-160 and a pseudo-square channel of dimension \( 5.8\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times5.9\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) for MOF303. The ultramicroporous feature can provide well confined environment for gas molecules. Particularly, the strategic adoption of the electronically polarized five-membered O,N-heterocycles ligands with additional functionalities, such as furan oxygens and N-termini of pyrazole groups, provides multiple potential electronegative binding sites for gas molecules. The periodical arrangement of these heteroatoms, akin to the quadrangle configuration of F atoms in SIFSIX-1-Cu pore channels, is in favor of maximizing the host–guest interactions and forming gas clusters through guest–guest interactions. Furthermore, combining the suitable pore dimension, such a regular layout of pore environment would favor the commensurate stacking of gas molecules.
Experimentally, the crystallinity and phase purity of the synthesized MIL-160 and MOF-303 were first investigated by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figs. S1–S3 in the ESM). The sharp diffraction peaks match well with the simulated diffraction patterns for both MIL-160 and MOF-303, verifying the high crystallinity and phase purity. Besides, the permanent porosity of two materials was investigated by \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption–desorption isotherms at \( 77\,\mathrm{~K~} \) (Fig. S4 in the ESM). Two materials exhibited
typical type-I adsorption behaviors, indicating the ultramicropores in the frameworks (Table S2 and Fig. S5 in the ESM). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of MIL-160 and MOF-303 were calculated to be 1110 and \(1343\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively, which were consistent with the previously reported values (Table S2 in the ESM). Further, the PXRD patterns as well as the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms of MIL-160 and MOF-303 remained intact after immersing in water for one week (Figs. S6 and S7 in the ESM), demonstrating no phase transitions and porous structure collapse occurred. The excellent stability is highly desirable in industrial process.
The confined pore environments imparted by combining suitable ultramicropores with periodically dispersed molecular recognition sites of the model MOFs prompt us to explore their potential for \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capture and purification. To begin with, the single-component adsorption isotherms of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\), and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) on MIL-160 and MOF-303 were collected under different temperatures (Figs. 2(a) and 2(b) and Figs. S8–S11 in the ESM). Before each test, the samples were immersed in methanol and activated at \(393\;\mathrm{K}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\). Surprisingly, MIL-160 and MOF-303 exhibit the ultrahigh \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptakes of 235 and \(195\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively, at \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) and 1.0 bar. The \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake on MIL-160 is much higher than the reported values by Li and Ma [42] (Fig. S30 in the ESM), which is resulted from the well-activated pore channels, and the \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake exceeds the top-performing materials reported so far (Fig. 2(c) and Fig. S12 in the ESM), such as the typically OMS-type MOFs of FJU-H8 \((224\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [18], coMOF-74 \((197\;\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [20], and ZJU-5 \((193\;\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [21]; functionality-type MOFs of NIX-1- \(\mathrm{Cu}(190\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [22], ZUL-100 \((118.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [43], and ZNU-1 \((76\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [44]; and recently-reported SNNU-27 \((182.4\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [38] and FJU-90 \((180\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [39]. Obviously, the \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity on MIL-160 sets a new benchmark for porous materials used for \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage. Exhilaratingly, MIL-160 and MOF-303 also showed topperforming \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage density of 0.63 and \(0.43\mathrm{~g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) derived from the exceptional \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake and modest pore volume (Fig. 2(d)). To the best of our knowledge, the storage density of MIL
160 outperforms the second place (lower than MOF-OH) compared to the benchmark MOFs including CAU-10-H \((0.392\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and CAU-10- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((0.46\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) [40, 45], implying that except for \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) clusters in MIL-160 pore channels, \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules may be commensurately stacked or compressed in the confined pore environment.
Detailed analysis of the adsorption properties of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) to gain insights into the potential of MIL-160 and MOF-303 for \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capture, the gas uptake of MIL-160 followed the trend: \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \((235\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\;>\;C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) \((108\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\;>\;\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \((107\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\), corresponding to \(8.7\;\;\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(3.8\;\;\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\), and \(3.8\;\;\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) per cell (Fig. 2(a)). Such a high trapping number of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (ca. 9) in one cell is for the first time observed in ultramicroporous MOFs. Impressively, the \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) isotherm exhibited a steep increase at low pressure regions, whereas the \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorbed amount grew slowly with the pressure increasing and ultimately formed two similar curves, which may be attributed to the fact that the uniformly arranged electronegative O atoms can provide preferential binding affinity for \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) over \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Besides, the gravimetric gas adsorption amount on MOF-303 followed a sequence of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \((195\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\;>\;C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) \((118\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\;>\;\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \((117\;\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) under the same conditions, corresponding to \(6.9\;\;\;\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(4.1\;\;\;\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\), and \(4.1\;\;\;\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) per cell (Fig. 2(b)). It is noteworthy that the \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) curves showed rapid and sharp increases at low pressure regions, while \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake nearly increased linearly with the pressure up to 1.0 bar, suggesting that the dual-nitrogen basic sites in the framework may favor the selective binding of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) with acidic H atoms over \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Additionally, an overlay of the adsorption and desorption branches with almost no hysteresis on two materials implies that the adsorption process was reversible and the materials can be easily regenerated under mild conditions.
The separation potential of two materials for equimolar binary mixtures of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) was evaluated by the ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) selectivity (Fig. S13 in the ESM). The selectivities of MIL-160 and MOF-303 at \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) and 1.0 bar were calculated to be 11 and 10.7 for \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), 9.7 and 10.8 for
\(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\), respectively. Based on IAST calculations, the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptakes from \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) mixtures can reach \(8.0\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) (Fig. 2(e) and Fig. S14 in the ESM). The exceptional performance with balanced \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) separation selectivities as well as the ultrahigh \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake outperformed most materials designed for the separation of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) mixtures [1, 9, 12, 13, 15, 24, 30]. Thus, with the suitable dimension and molecular recognition sites, MIL-160 not only breaks the record in \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake capacity, but also strikes an excellent selectivity balance between the two pairs of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) mixtures.
To decode the high density and selective uptake of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in the two MOFs, the heat of adsorption \(\left(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\right)\) for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) was calculated. The \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values of MIL-160 and MOF-303 follow the same trend of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}>C_{2}H_{4}>C O_{2}}\), indicating the stronger affinity of these two MOFs for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Fig. 2(f)). Meanwhile, MIL-160 and MOF-303 show the modest \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) with 35.1 and \(33.3\ \mathrm{kJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}\), respectively. These values are obviously lower than that of many well-known MOFs, such as NKMOF-1-Ni \((60.3\ \mathrm{kJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}})\) [14], UTSA-300 \((57.6\ \mathrm{kJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}})\) [1], and SIFSIX-series materials (up to \(41.9\ \mathrm{kJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}\)) [31]. This indicates the model MOFs can achieve a reversible adsorption/desorption process under moderate conditions and afford great reduction in energy intake for adsorbent regeneration. Besides, the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) on two MOFs slightly increase with the increasing \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptakes, which hints that the intermolecular interactions contribute an important role in efficiently commensurate stacking of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules in the pore channels. This \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) commensurate stacking phenomenon along with \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) increase on \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption is similar with that in SIFSIX-1-Cu and \(\mathrm{CAU}{-}10{-}\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) [22, 40, 41]. Therefore, the concomitant OMSs-free and commensurate stacking within two Al-MOFs culminated in achieving ultrahigh \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage density but with moderate \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\).
To delve deeper into the underlying mechanism for the ultrahigh \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity and separation performance, grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations and density functional theory (DFT) calculations were conducted because the
Apparently, the occurrence of multiple host–guest interactions is observed on two materials (Figs. 3(a) and 3(d)). Besides the \(\mathrm{C-H^{6+}{\cdot}O^{6-}}\) and \(\mathrm{C-H^{6+}{\cdot}N^{6-}}\) interactions (ranging from 2.819 to \(3.632\ \mathrm{\AA}\)) between \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule and the electronegative O and N atoms from the carboxylate group, furan, and pyrazole units, the host–guest interactions are also supplemented by parallel \(\pi\cdots\pi\) interactions between the \(\pi\)-electrons of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and furan (pyrazole) rings. In addition, there exists one \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule being fully surrounded by other \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules within each unit cell of two MOFs (Figs. 3(b) and 3(e)). This indicates the formation of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) clusters due to the radial-like \(\pi\cdots\pi\) interactions and \(\mathrm{C-H^{6+}{\cdot}C^{6-}}\) dipole–dipole interactions between \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules, which were not unveiled in previous work [42]. Thus, each \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule is stabilized by multiple host–guest and guest–guest interactions in a perfectly cooperative manner within the O- and N-rich channels of MIL-160 and MOF-303, affording their ultrahigh \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake capacities.
In comparison, the two MOFs can only accommodate with four molecules of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) per cell (Figs. 3(c) and 3(f) and
Fig. S23 in the ESM), which is attributed to the weaker and less synergistic host–guest (guest–guest) interactions. Additionally, the corresponding binding energies of \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) were calculated to be 35.8 and \( 33.1\;\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \) for MIL-160 and MOF-303, respectively, higher than those of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) (28.9 and \( 30.2\ \mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \) ) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) (29.3 and \( 31.5\;\;\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \) ). The above theoretical observations coherently support the separation potential of these two MOFs for simultaneous separation of \( C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) mixtures.
To illustrate the separation performances of two materials, transient breakthrough simulations were firstly conducted for equimolar \( 50/50\;\;\;\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) mixtures under ambient conditions (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)) [46, 47]. The results presented that the clear separation for two mixtures can be achieved with high efficiency. The calculated \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capture amount on MIL-160 can reach up to \( 7.3\;\mathrm{\mol{\cdot}k g^{-1}} \) from two mixtures, consistent with the simulated \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) recovery amount with high purity (Fig. 4(c) and Fig. S24 in the ESM). Such a record high \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capture amount promoted us to evaluate the actual separation performances. Equally astonishing, excellent separation performances and agreement between the experimental and simulated breakthrough curves were achieved (Figs. S25 and S26 in the ESM). The retention time of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) in MIL-160 packed column was \( 161\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) , which is not only 2.4 times longer than that of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) \( (68\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}}) \) but also an unprecedented value up to now (Fig. 4(d)). Similarly, MOF-303 showed relatively more rapid breakthrough of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) with selective retention of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) (retention time of 38 and \( 87\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) , respectively, Fig. 4(e)). The separation factors were calculated to be 10.1 and 2.1, respectively, on MIL-160 and MOF-303 for \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) separation. It is worth noting that the trapping amounts of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) from the dynamic adsorption of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) mixtures on MIL-160 and MOF-303 are 201 and \( 173\;\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \) , 86% and \( 88\% \) of the saturated uptakes from the single-component isotherms at \( 298\;\mathrm{K} \) and 1.0 bar. Remarkably, MIL-160 showed both the highest breakthrough time and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capture amount among all of the top-performing materials under the same mixed gas flow rate of \( 2\ \mathrm{mL{\cdot}min^{-1}} \) at \( 298\ \mathrm{K} \) , i.e., ATC-Cu \( (132\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) and \( 103\;\;\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \) ) [48], FJI-H8-Me \( (89\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) and \( 194\;\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \) ) [49], and SNU-45 \( (79\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) and \( 115\;\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \) ) [29]
(Fig. 4(f) and Fig. S26 in the ESM).
For \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) separation, the eluted time for \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) : 157 and \( 92\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) in MIL-160 and MOF-303, respectively, was much longer than that of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) : 71 and \( 42\ \mathrm{min{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) in MIL-160 and MOF-303, respectively. These observations indicate that the ultrahigh \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) uptakes and good selectivity of MIL-160 and MOF-303 were well maintained under mixed-gas dynamic conditions. The two MOFs also showed a significantly higher elution time of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) than that of CAU-10-H (Fig. S28 in the ESM), which further confirmed the superior \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) adsorption performance of the two MOFs. Notably, among all candidates, MIL-160 has the greatest potential in overcoming the problem of the trade-off between \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) uptake and selectivity. And the cyclic breakthrough tests revealed that MIL-160 retained the excellent \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capture performance after six cycles, validating the good recyclability of MIL-160 for \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) separations (Fig. S29 in the ESM). Taken together, these results demonstrate that the challenging \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) separations can be satisfactorily addressed using MIL-160 and MOF-303 under ambient conditions.
## 3 Conclusions
To summarize, the combination of pore confinement and commensurate stacking effects was employed as a powerful strategy to efficiently trap \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) from both \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \). Owing to the suitable 1D channels lined by periodically interspersed molecular recognition sites, MIL-160 and MOF-303 exhibit high \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) affinity, especially MIL-160 registered the highest \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) capacity of \( 235\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1} \) among the reported \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) selective physisorbents (including OMS types). Conversely, only moderate amounts of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) were adsorbed onto the model adsorbents, thus giving both high \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) selectivity. Computational simulations confirmed that the currently occurred commensurate stacking of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) in the confirmed ultramicropores through multiple host–guest and guest–guest interactions would account for the ultrahigh \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) storage capacity. Through this commensurate stacking strategy,
the ability of the model MOFs to fruitfully couple record-high \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake and outstanding selectivity over \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) was highlighted. This work would not only provide insights into the strategy of the cogeneration of gas cluster and commensurate stacking through maximization of host–guest and guest–guest interactions within the confined pores containing arrayed binding sites, but also pave new avenues for pore engineering in gas adsorption and separation.
